---
import {blackhole,length,SOLAR_MASS} from '../lib/science.mjs';import {sci} from '../lib/planets.mjs';
const units=[['Solar masses (M☉)',SOLAR_MASS],['Jupiter masses',1.89813e27],['Earth masses',5.9722e24],['Kilograms',1]];const r=blackhole(1).radius;
// Worked values for the masses people look up most; same function as the calculator.
const examples=[['Earth',5.9722e24],['Jupiter',1.89813e27],['The Sun',SOLAR_MASS]].map(([name,kg])=>({name,kg,radius:blackhole(kg/SOLAR_MASS).radius}));
---
<form class="quick-calc" data-schwarzschild><h2>Schwarzschild radius calculator</h2><p>Enter any mass. The event horizon radius of a non-rotating black hole is rₛ = 2GM/c².</p><div class="quick-fields"><label class="field"><span class="field-name">Mass</span><input type="number" name="mass" value="1" min="0" step="any" inputmode="decimal" required/></label><label class="field"><span class="field-name">Unit</span><select name="unit">{units.map(([label,kg])=><option value={kg}>{label}</option>)}</select></label></div><div class="readouts"><div class="readout"><small>Schwarzschild radius</small><strong data-out="radius">{length(r)}</strong></div><div class="readout"><small>Horizon diameter</small><strong data-out="diameter">{length(2*r)}</strong></div><div class="readout"><small>Mass in kilograms</small><strong data-out="mass">{sci(SOLAR_MASS)} kg</strong></div></div><p class="status" role="status" data-status></p></form>
<h2>Schwarzschild radius of the Sun, Earth and Jupiter</h2><p>The Schwarzschild radius is how small a mass would have to be squeezed for its escape speed to reach the speed of light. None of these objects will become a black hole; the values show how the radius grows in step with mass.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Object</th><th scope="col">Mass</th><th scope="col">Schwarzschild radius</th><th scope="col">Event horizon diameter</th></tr></thead><tbody>{examples.map(e=><tr><th scope="row">{e.name}</th><td>{sci(e.kg)} kg</td><td>{length(e.radius)}</td><td>{length(2*e.radius)}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div><p class="worked">Calculated with rₛ = 2GM/c². For real black holes such as Sagittarius A*, see the worked examples below or open the <a href="/labs/black-hole-radius/">black hole event horizon simulator</a>.</p>
<script>
import {blackhole,length,SOLAR_MASS} from '../lib/science.mjs';import {sci} from '../lib/planets.mjs';
document.querySelectorAll<HTMLFormElement>('[data-schwarzschild]').forEach(form=>{const out=(k:string,v:string)=>form.querySelector(`[data-out="${k}"]`)!.textContent=v,status=form.querySelector('[data-status]')!;const update=()=>{const kg=Number((form.elements.namedItem('mass') as HTMLInputElement).value)*Number((form.elements.namedItem('unit') as HTMLSelectElement).value);if(!Number.isFinite(kg)||kg<=0){status.textContent='Enter a mass greater than zero.';return;}status.textContent='';const r=blackhole(kg/SOLAR_MASS).radius;out('radius',length(r));out('diameter',length(2*r));out('mass',`${sci(kg)} kg`);};form.addEventListener('input',update);form.addEventListener('submit',e=>{e.preventDefault();update();});});
</script>
